}1 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix},\\,{E}_{2}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Notice that ${E}_{1}$ and ${E}_{2}$ are both elementary matrices.\n\nFirst, we need to find ${E}_{1}^{-1}.$ We write down ${E}_{1}$ and the identity matrix $I$ side-by-side (as shown below) and apply an elementary row operation that transforms the left matrix into the identity matrix:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[{E}_{1}\\,|\\,I\\,] & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix} &  & {R}_{1}:={R}_{1}+{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n{E}_{1}^{-1}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nNow, we can find the product ${E}_{1}^{-1}{E}_{2}:$\n\n$$\n{E}_{1}^{-1}{E}_{2}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186551296 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115244 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "6c56b3e9-cf23-5e1e-8c7d-c474059c9a9d" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Inverse of an Elementary 2x2 Matrix"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "726b610b-966f-5fad-8937-be3c12d0601d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}1 & -4 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186550654} {:answer/id #uuid "77d890b8-7322-5242-a284-9e74f77fee01" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-4 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186550671} {:answer/id #uuid "d0ae9d64-3c04-543e-9bad-0f91c19f44f2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 4 & 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186550928} {:answer/id #uuid "db2ac3cb-cacf-5f7f-8f49-0a6d597fce81" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 4\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186550963} {:answer/id #uuid "f6d47fd4-271e-59b7-9dfb-e64e565385a2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}1 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186551029}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d0ae9d64-3c04-543e-9bad-0f91c19f44f2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 4 & 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186550928} :answer-field/id #uuid "3af9e33e-f08c-574c-97d5-344ef1178cc0" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186551102}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "7effc142-a727-5443-a7a6-2e2db67c4b1f" :question/math-academy-id "q-32008" :question/problem "Find the inverse of the elementary matrix \n$S=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ -4 & 1\\end{aligned}].$" :question/worked-solution "We write down $S$ and the identity matrix $I$ side-by-side (as shown below) and apply an elementary row operation that transforms the left matrix into the identity matrix:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[S\\,|\\,I\\,] & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ -4 & 1 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] &  & {R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+4{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 4 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe inverse is obtained on the right-hand side of our big matrix after the transformation. Therefore, \n\n$$\n{S}^{-1}=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 4 & 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115841 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115245 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "dd12bc7b-1b5d-5af2-bcef-c9d0fda7d8fc" :knowledge-point/title "Computing a Product Involving the Inverse of an Elementary 2x2 Matrix"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3d5cf453-a524-596c-a71c-3cc10ff9251a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186550509} {:answer/id #uuid "9f574a63-4cd5-5ba3-aee2-b3411a92aab5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}15 & 7 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186550776} {:answer/id #uuid "a1f3902b-ad8b-5c0c-8a37-a910843374dd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186550785} {:answer/id #uuid "cb2def03-42ec-5d22-9a67-872732233e9a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}15 & -7 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186550915} {:answer/id #uuid "d9d061de-733c-5ff8-b08f-66b5ba33a70c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}15 & 2 \\\\ 7 & 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186550957}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9f574a63-4cd5-5ba3-aee2-b3411a92aab5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}15 & 7 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186550776} :answer-field/id #uuid "4eb2360f-9c37-5242-a36d-bf3f7078ccc0" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186113983}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "e24eefb5-7086-59e4-b488-5ab843ea61ec" :question/math-academy-id "q-42696" :question/problem "Find the product ${E}_{1}^{-1}{E}_{2}$, where\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{E}_{1} & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & -7 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}],\\,{E}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Notice that ${E}_{1}$ and ${E}_{2}$ are both elementary matrices.\n\nFirst, we need to find ${E}_{1}^{-1}.$ We write down ${E}_{1}$ and the identity matrix $I$ side-by-side (as shown below) and apply an elementary row operation that transforms the left matrix into the identity matrix:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[{E}_{1}\\,|\\,I\\,] & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & -7 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] &  & {R}_{1}:={R}_{1}+7{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 1 & 7 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n{E}_{1}^{-1}=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 7 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nNow, we can find the product ${E}_{1}^{-1}{E}_{2}:$\n\n$$\n{E}_{1}^{-1}{E}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 7 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}15 & 7 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{aligned}]\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186436529 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115243 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "853a04b2-591a-51c2-b80f-73f559355d9f" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying an Elementary 2x2 Matrix"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f564662a-a586-5cf0-983d-a463cece18c9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186474828} {:answer/id #uuid "cd9ec697-d62c-5cd9-a7e3-d0be9a26170b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186474829}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f564662a-a586-5cf0-983d-a463cece18c9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186474828} :answer-field/id #uuid "9c3c4731-0b36-5964-b452-fbb2af92efe4" :answer-field/key "M" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186475340} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "58c9ad47-9cc2-5588-b29e-efbaec72d300" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186474830} {:answer/id #uuid "6d6dd6fc-300a-5536-a4f8-6bf0bd3b7419" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186474831}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "58c9ad47-9cc2-5588-b29e-efbaec72d300" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186474830} :answer-field/id #uuid "1f81fd52-e05c-55c9-8525-5b6cb62e9802" :answer-field/key "N" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186475341} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c47f6210-eb6e-572b-8727-62ace0a342a3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186474832} {:answer/id #uuid "82ef391b-48b6-5a1e-b086-4012d85bb009" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186474833}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c47f6210-eb6e-572b-8727-62ace0a342a3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186474832} :answer-field/id #uuid "1c0c485d-839f-59be-8e63-a184b74dc721" :answer-field/key "P" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186475342}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "bb398bcb-fa77-5232-9d5f-318cb8cb3320" :question/math-academy-id "q-42704" :question/problem "Which of the following are elementary matrices?\n\n$$\nM=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{bmatrix},\\,N=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 5 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{bmatrix},\\,P=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & -2 \\\\ 4 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nSelect all that apply. If none apply, leave all boxes unchecked." :question/worked-solution "An elementary matrix is a matrix obtained from the identity matrix by applying exactly one elementary row operation. \n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our matrices.\n\n- The matrices $M$, $N$ and $P$ are not elementary matrices because they can't be obtained from ${I}_{2}$ by applying only one elementary row operation."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186551262 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115243 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "853a04b2-591a-51c2-b80f-73f559355d9f" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying an Elementary 2x2 Matrix"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6f096b5f-39c2-585d-9076-e381a97ad95f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$T$ and $U$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186550644} {:answer/id #uuid "95ac74e0-7781-595b-b1cb-5685696d0923" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$T$ and $X$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186550745} {:answer/id #uuid "9c9ae38d-468e-5c1b-a879-895b482ed7aa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$X$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186550766} {:answer/id #uuid "ace569a1-cdae-5f8a-b5cb-eb5a89edde5d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$U$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186550833} {:answer/id #uuid "c605cbc3-363f-5199-936d-cf502df10aa1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$U$ and $X$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186550902}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c605cbc3-363f-5199-936d-cf502df10aa1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$U$ and $X$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186550902} :answer-field/id #uuid "b0f0fef4-e8e5-5e33-982a-88aa1db63724" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186551171}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "3a9d38f9-b939-5805-88eb-ef96ffddb725" :question/math-academy-id "q-42754" :question/problem "Which of the following are elementary matrices?\n\n$$\nT=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 3\\end{aligned}],\\,U=[\\begin{aligned}9 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}],\\,X=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]\n$$" :question/worked-solution "An elementary matrix is a matrix obtained from the identity matrix by applying exactly one elementary row operation. \n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our matrices.\n\n- The matrix $T$ is not an elementary matrix because it can't be obtained from ${I}_{2}$ by applying only one elementary row operation.\n\n- The matrix $U$ is an elementary matrix because it can be obtained from ${I}_{2}$ by applying the elementary row operation ${R}_{1}:=9{R}_{1},$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{I}_{2} & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] &  & {R}_{1}:=9{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}9 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =U\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- The matrix $X$ is an elementary matrix because it can be obtained from ${I}_{2}$ by applying the elementary row operation ${R}_{1}:={R}_{1}+3{R}_{2},$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{I}_{2} & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] &  & {R}_{1}:={R}_{1}+3{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =X\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, only $U$ and $X$ are elementary matrices."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186551294 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115243 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "853a04b2-591a-51c2-b80f-73f559355d9f" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying an Elementary 2x2 Matrix"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2009ffbb-834a-5c7a-8a8b-959aa0b24db2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$N$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186550438} {:answer/id #uuid "40c3d139-ddd9-5cf3-80a9-cb1261eea249" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$H$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186550516} {:answer/id #uuid "44c29b3a-55ca-586c-873f-1a2bc7582a6d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$K$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186550528} {:answer/id #uuid "65e51982-290b-5109-8387-cd14e01378eb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$K$ and $N$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186550619} {:answer/id #uuid "c4a6f7a8-a40b-5e83-8bb3-7321150a3ccc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$H$ and $K$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186550900}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2009ffbb-834a-5c7a-8a8b-959aa0b24db2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$N$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186550438} :answer-field/id #uuid "f4e59a97-7099-5d85-88ab-f4f7fe7124bb" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186551222}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "7abe0f71-31aa-5c86-a7a0-fa2df71ee9ab" :question/math-academy-id "q-42756" :question/problem "Which of the following are **not** elementary matrices?\n\n$$\nH=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 5 & 1\\end{aligned}],\\,K=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}],\\,N=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 0\\end{aligned}]\n$$" :question/worked-solution "An elementary matrix is a matrix obtained from the identity matrix by applying exactly one elementary row operation. \n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our matrices.\n\n- The matrix $H$ is an elementary matrix because it can be obtained from ${I}_{2}$ by applying the elementary row operation ${R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+5{R}_{1},$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{I}_{2} & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] &  & {R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+5{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 5 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =H\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- The matrix $K$ is an elementary matrix because it can be obtained from ${I}_{2}$ by applying the elementary row operation ${R}_{1}↔{R}_{2},$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{I}_{2} & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] &  & {R}_{1}↔{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =K\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- The matrix $N$ is not an elementary matrix because it can't be obtained from ${I}_{2}$ by applying only one elementary row operation.\n\nTherefore, only $N$ is not an elementary matrix."}]}
